| Emitting something that is not a particle is a mistake by the *sender*,
| and it reads the same wherever it was pointed. The question is asked once,
| by `emit` itself, before the target is even looked at.
|
| It was asked five times before 2026-08-28 — twice by `core`, once by `to
| this`, in two different wordings — and not at all on the module path,
| which could not ask: a module reads `_class`, finds none, and cannot tell
| "not a particle" from "a class I don't handle", so `emit 5 to math` did
| nothing at all while `emit 5 to this` failed.
|
| The failure is a value like any other: the frame ends and answers with an
| `Exception`. Nothing is terminated.
link "native_modules/math.so" as math

Viathis {} => {
    emit 5 to this get r
    return Reached {}
}
Viacore {} => {
    emit 5 to core get r
    return Reached {}
}
Viamodule {} => {
    emit 5 to math get r
    return Reached {}
}

emit Viathis {} to this get a
emit Viacore {} to this get b
emit Viamodule {} to this get c

assert a ∈ Exception
assert b ∈ Exception
assert c ∈ Exception

| One question, so one answer: the same message from all three.
assert a.message = b.message
assert b.message = c.message
assert a.message = "emit requires a particle — an object with a '_class' field — found a number"

| Not just numbers: a plain object is not a particle either, since there is
| no `_class` to dispatch on.
Plain {} => {
    emit { value = 1 } to math get r
    return Reached {}
}
emit Plain {} to this get d
assert d ∈ Exception
assert d.message = "emit requires a particle — an object with a '_class' field — found an object"

| And this is a different question from "nobody handles that class", which
| is still null rather than a failure — sending a particle is not a demand.
emit Nobody {} to math get e
assert e = null
